--- THE BLOCKER: ONE PRE-EXISTING TEST THAT T-004 CANNOT SATISFY ---

FAILING TEST
  mcp/sdd-forge-mcp/tests/path-security/list-guarded-files-diagnostics.test.ts:463
  "TEST-006 (AC-006, BL-003): none of the 3 existing call sites was changed
   to opt into the diagnostics function"

It loops over 3 source files -- report-lookup.ts, quality-report.ts,
review-ticket.ts -- and for each asserts BOTH:
  (1) source.includes("listGuardedFiles(root, relDir)")            must be true
  (2) !source.includes("listGuardedFilesWithDiagnostics")           must be true
with the failure message on (2) reading, verbatim:
  "... must NOT opt into listGuardedFilesWithDiagnostics in this task
   (requirements.md Non-goals; T-004 owns report-lookup.ts's opt-in)"

The assertion that actually fired is (1):
  AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: mcp/sdd-forge-mcp/src/parsers/report-lookup.ts
  must still call listGuardedFiles(root, relDir) unchanged

WHY IT IS UNSATISFIABLE FOR T-004, NOT A DEFECT IN THIS IMPLEMENTATION
design.md's API/Contract Plan quotes anyFileContainingWithDiagnostics'
implementation literally, and its second statement is
    const { files, errors } = listGuardedFilesWithDiagnostics(root, relDir);
So T-004's required implementation necessarily (a) contains the string
"listGuardedFilesWithDiagnostics" in report-lookup.ts, violating (2), and
(b) no longer contains the literal "listGuardedFiles(root, relDir)" in that
file, violating (1). Both halves of the loop's body contradict T-004 for
report-lookup.ts specifically. There is no implementation of design.md's
quoted code that passes this test; the only ways to "pass" it would be to
keep a dead listGuardedFiles call or to obscure the identifier, both of which
are gaming a test rather than satisfying it.

design.md agrees with the implementation, not with the test. Its
report-lookup.ts section says only:
    "quality-report.ts/review-ticket.ts are UNCHANGED (Non-goals; they keep
     calling listGuardedFiles directly, never
     listGuardedFilesWithDiagnostics)."
naming exactly TWO files that must not opt in -- report-lookup.ts is
deliberately not among them, because report-lookup.ts is where T-004's opt-in
lives. The test's own message says the same thing ("T-004 owns
report-lookup.ts's opt-in"); it simply asserts the T-003-era scope over all 3
files without re-scoping itself when T-004 lands.

WHAT WAS NOT DONE, AND WHY
This task's constraints forbid weakening or rewriting an existing test to make
a change pass, and require stopping and reporting instead when one
legitimately must change. That is this case, so the test was left BYTE-
UNCHANGED and the red result is reported here rather than edited away. The
minimal correct fix is recorded in the implementation report for a human to
approve; it is NOT applied in this commit.

